{%- comment -%}
  ============================================================================
  Lookbook — serialises `lookbook` metaobject entries and mounts the renderer.

  This is the reusable entry point: it owns the field keys and the payload, so a
  lookbook can be dropped into any section, template or other snippet, not only
  sections/lookbook.liquid.

    {% render 'lookbook', uid: 'home-lookbook', heading: 'Shop the look' %}

  ACCEPTS (every one optional)
    uid                 unique id for the JSON script tag. Pass section.id from
                        a section. REQUIRED if two lookbooks share a page —
                        matching ids would make both mount the same payload.
    entry_source        'all' (default) for every Active entry, or 'selected' to
                        use the `entries` param.
    entries             a metaobject_list, used only when entry_source is
                        'selected'.
    entries_limit       max entries when entry_source is 'all'. Default 6.
    products_limit      max products per look, both sources. Default 12.
    heading             inline_richtext section heading. Omit for no heading.
    heading_size        'h0' | 'h1' | 'h2'. Default 'h1'.
    sub_heading         plain text under the section heading. Optional.
    description         richtext (already HTML) under the sub heading. Optional.
    show_sub_heading    show each ENTRY's sub heading. Default true.
    show_description    show each ENTRY's description. Default true.
    masonry_row_height  Masonry row unit in px; scales the card heights.
                        Default 120.
    product_cta_label   button text over each product image. NO default — omit
                        it, or pass an empty string, and no button is drawn.
    show_notices        render the theme-editor diagnostics. Default true.

  RELATING LOOKS TO ONE PRODUCT (used by sections/lookbook-related.liquid)
    containing_product  a product. When given, only looks that already feature
                        that product are rendered — the metaobject IS the
                        relationship, so no extra field is needed to say which
                        looks belong to a product.
    exclude_product     a product to leave out of every look's product list.
                        Normally the same product, so a product page does not
                        recommend the page you are already on.
    force_template      overrides each entry's own Template choice. A product
                        page wants one consistent layout regardless of how each
                        look was set up for the lookbook page.

  WHICH PRODUCTS (per entry)
  Each entry carries its own "Products Source" choice list:
    manual      the products picked in that entry's product list field.
    collection  the products in that entry's Collection field.
  Either way the list is capped by `products_limit`.

  Manual selection is the default. An entry only pulls from its collection when
  Products Source explicitly says so, so entries saved before that field existed
  — and entries where the choice was left empty — keep showing picked products.

  WHICH TEMPLATE (per entry)
  The entry's "Template" choice list is passed through untouched and resolved in
  src/lookbook/templates/index.jsx, which normalises the value and falls back to
  Default. Add a template there, not here.

  FIELD KEYS are declared once in the {% liquid %} block below. Field keys are
  not the same as the labels shown in admin — expand a field in the metaobject
  definition to see its key. Renaming a field's label does NOT rename its key,
  which is why the products field is resolved from two candidate keys. If the
  keys below are wrong, the section renders with empty text and the theme editor
  shows a notice saying so.

  Only entries with status Active appear. Draft entries are not exposed to the
  storefront, so they will not show here or on the live site.
  ============================================================================
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # ---- Field keys: the only place to edit if a key differs ----
  assign key_title = 'title'
  assign key_subheading = 'sub_heading'
  assign key_description = 'discreption'
  assign key_template = 'template'
  assign key_collection = 'collection'
  assign key_products_source = 'products_source'

  # The product list field was created under an earlier label and later
  # relabelled "Select Products". Shopify keeps the original key on a rename, so
  # both are tried per entry; drop `key_products_legacy` once no definition
  # uses it any more.
  assign key_products = 'select_products'
  assign key_products_legacy = 'top_3_field'

  # ---- Parameter defaults ----
  # `allow_false: true` matters on the checkboxes: without it a deliberate
  # `false` from the caller would be treated as "not supplied" and flip to true.
  assign uid = uid | default: 'lookbook'
  assign entry_source = entry_source | default: 'all'
  assign entries_limit = entries_limit | default: 6
  assign products_limit = products_limit | default: 12
  assign heading_size = heading_size | default: 'h1'
  assign show_sub_heading = show_sub_heading | default: true, allow_false: true
  assign show_description = show_description | default: true, allow_false: true
  assign masonry_row_height = masonry_row_height | default: 120
  assign show_notices = show_notices | default: true, allow_false: true

  # Counts entries actually emitted. The entries loop can now skip an entry
  # (it may not feature `containing_product`), so `forloop.last` no longer
  # marks the last one WRITTEN and cannot drive the commas between them —
  # a skipped final entry would leave a trailing comma and break the JSON.
  # Both loops below emit a leading comma from the second item onward instead.
  assign emitted_entries = 0

  # Either every Active entry, or only the ones passed in.
  # A picked list is already exactly what the merchant wants, in their order, so
  # the limit is effectively lifted rather than applied a second time.
  if entry_source == 'selected'
    assign lookbook_entries = entries
    assign entry_limit = 50
  else
    assign lookbook_entries = shop.metaobjects.lookbook.values
    assign entry_limit = entries_limit
  endif
-%}

{%- capture entries_json -%}
[
{%- for entry in lookbook_entries -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Rich text is awkward to render from Liquid on a metaobject field: `.value`
    returns the document tree (which stringifies into {"type"=>"root", ...}),
    and outputting the field object itself produces nothing at all.

    So the tree is handed to React as-is and rendered there — it is a plain
    hash, so `json` serialises it correctly. `metafield_tag` is sent alongside
    as a fallback for the case where the tree comes back empty; the component
    prefers the tree, so a Liquid error from that filter can never be displayed.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture description_html %}{{ entry[key_description] | metafield_tag }}{% endcapture -%}

  {%- liquid
    # ---- Resolve this entry's product list ----
    # Matched with `contains 'collection'` rather than an exact string so the
    # choice list values can read however the merchant wrote them ("Collection",
    # "From collection", ...). Anything else — including an empty choice — means
    # manual selection, which is the intended default.
    assign entry_source_value = entry[key_products_source].value | default: '' | downcase
    assign entry_collection = entry[key_collection].value

    assign entry_products = entry[key_products].value
    if entry_products == blank
      assign entry_products = entry[key_products_legacy].value
    endif

    # How many images each product contributes. Only the "Masonry - Product
    # Images" template draws more than one, and sending three per product to a
    # template that shows one would triple the payload for nothing.
    #
    # This is a loose match on the raw choice value, not a mirror of the alias
    # table in src/lookbook/templates/index.jsx — keeping the two in exact sync
    # would mean maintaining the same list twice. Guessing wrong is safe in the
    # direction that matters: too few images makes a group shrink, which is
    # already the defined behaviour for a product that has few images.
    # `force_template` wins over the entry's own choice, so a caller that needs
    # one consistent layout (a product page) is not at the mercy of how each
    # look happened to be configured for the lookbook page.
    assign entry_template_raw = entry[key_template].value | default: ''
    if force_template != blank
      assign entry_template_raw = force_template
    endif

    assign entry_template_value = entry_template_raw | downcase | replace: '-', ' ' | replace: '_', ' '
    assign product_image_limit = 1
    if entry_template_value contains 'product image'
      assign product_image_limit = 3
    endif

    # One cap for both sources, so the caller decides how long a look runs
    # whether the products were hand-picked or pulled from a collection.
    assign entry_products_limit = products_limit
    assign render_products = true

    if entry_source_value contains 'collection'
      if entry_collection == blank
        # Falling back to the picked products here would silently contradict the
        # source the merchant chose, so the look renders without products and
        # the theme editor points at the entry instead.
        assign render_products = false
        assign has_missing_collection = true
      else
        assign entry_products = entry_collection.products
      endif
    endif

    # ---- Does this look feature `containing_product`? ----
    # With no product to relate to, every entry qualifies and this costs one
    # comparison. Collection-sourced looks are matched through the product's own
    # collections rather than by scanning the collection's products: that reads
    # a list the product already carries, and stays correct for collections
    # larger than the 50 products Liquid will hand back. A look set to Collection
    # with no collection picked renders no products, so it features none — the
    # products still in its picked list must not match it.
    assign entry_matches = true
    if containing_product != blank
      assign entry_matches = false
      if entry_source_value contains 'collection'
        if entry_collection != blank
          for related_collection in containing_product.collections
            if related_collection.handle == entry_collection.handle
              assign entry_matches = true
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif
      else
        for candidate in entry_products
          if candidate.id == containing_product.id
            assign entry_matches = true
            break
          endif
        endfor
      endif
    endif

    assign include_entry = false
    if entry_matches and emitted_entries < entry_limit
      assign include_entry = true
    endif
  -%}
  {%- if include_entry -%}
  {%- if emitted_entries > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
  {%- assign emitted_entries = emitted_entries | plus: 1 -%}
  {
    "id": {{ entry.system.id | json }},
    "title": {{ entry[key_title].value | default: '' | json }},
    "subHeading": {{ entry[key_subheading].value | default: '' | json }},
    "descriptionTree": {{ entry[key_description].value | json }},
    "descriptionHtml": {{ description_html | strip | json }},
    {%- comment -%}
      `template` is passed through raw — normalising and falling back to Default
      is the renderer's job, so a choice added in admin before its component
      exists cannot drop the look off the page.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    "template": {{ entry_template_raw | json }},
    "products": [
      {%- if render_products -%}
      {%- assign emitted_products = 0 -%}
      {%- for product in entry_products -%}
        {%- liquid
          # Counted rather than using `limit:`, because a product may be skipped
          # and the cap should mean "this many shown", not "this many examined".
          assign skip_product = false
          if exclude_product != blank and product.id == exclude_product.id
            assign skip_product = true
          endif
        -%}
        {%- if emitted_products >= entry_products_limit -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- unless skip_product -%}
        {%- if emitted_products > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
        {%- assign emitted_products = emitted_products | plus: 1 -%}
        {
          "id": {{ product.id | json }},
          "title": {{ product.title | json }},
          "url": {{ product.url | json }},
          {%- comment -%}
            A product carries only what the cards actually draw: a title, a link
            and its pictures. Price, compare-at price and availability used to be
            serialised here and were dropped once the overlay stopped showing
            them — the shape of the payload should say what the section renders.

            To bring price back, add a price member here rather than reaching
            for it in the component alone — it is not in the payload to reach
            for. The `money` filter is required when you do, because the shop's
            currency format lives in Liquid and is not reachable from
            JavaScript: pipe product.price through `money | strip_html | json`.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            An array even when it holds one image, so every template reads
            products the same way. `product.images` is ordered as the merchant
            arranged the media in admin, and its first entry is the featured
            image. A product with no media at all yields [], which the renderer
            draws as the empty placeholder.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          "images": [
            {%- for p_image in product.images limit: product_image_limit -%}
              {%- capture p_srcset -%}
                {{ p_image | image_url: width: 300 }} 300w,
                {{ p_image | image_url: width: 500 }} 500w,
                {{ p_image | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
                {{ p_image | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w
              {%- endcapture -%}
              {
                "src": {{ p_image | image_url: width: 500 | json }},
                "srcset": {{ p_srcset | strip_newlines | strip | json }},
                "alt": {{ p_image.alt | default: product.title | default: '' | json }},
                "width": {{ p_image.width | json }},
                "height": {{ p_image.height | json }}
              }{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ]
        }
        {%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    ]
  }
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture payload -%}
{
  "heading": {{ heading | default: '' | json }},
  "headingSize": {{ heading_size | json }},
  "subHeading": {{ sub_heading | default: '' | json }},
  "description": {{ description | default: '' | json }},
  "showSubHeading": {{ show_sub_heading | json }},
  "showDescription": {{ show_description | json }},
  "masonryRowHeight": {{ masonry_row_height | json }},
  {%- comment -%}
    Passed through without a `default` filter on purpose: Liquid cannot tell an
    empty string from an unset param, so a default here would make an
    intentionally blank label in the theme editor spring back to a button.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  "productCtaLabel": {{ product_cta_label | default: '' | json }},
  "entries": {{ entries_json }}
}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  # A closing script tag is neutralised in case a merchant pastes markup into
  # the rich text field — it would otherwise close the tag below early and break
  # the page.
  #
  # The needle is assembled from two halves so those characters never appear
  # literally in this file. Written out, Theme Check's HTML parser treats them
  # as closing the <script> element and fails with a LiquidHTMLSyntaxError,
  # which stops it parsing — and therefore checking — the rest of the snippet.
  #
  # The backslash below must survive into the output: `<\/script>` is a legal
  # JSON string escape that the HTML parser will not read as a closing tag.
  # Shopify's Liquid keeps it, because its lexer takes the raw text between the
  # quotes and never unescapes. Do not "tidy" this into a templating language
  # that does unescape string literals (liquidjs does) — there the two halves
  # collapse to the same value, `replace` becomes a no-op, and the guard is
  # silently gone.
  assign script_close = '</scr' | append: 'ipt>'
  assign script_close_safe = '<\/scr' | append: 'ipt>'
-%}
<script type="application/json" id="LookbookData-{{ uid }}">
  {{- payload | replace: script_close, script_close_safe -}}
</script>

<div data-lookbook="LookbookData-{{ uid }}"></div>

{%- if request.design_mode and show_notices -%}
  {%- liquid
    # `.first` returns nothing on the metaobject values array, so probe the
    # first entry with a one-iteration loop instead. Getting this wrong made
    # the notice below fire even when the fields were resolving fine.
    assign fields_matched = false
    for probe_entry in lookbook_entries limit: 1
      if probe_entry[key_title] != nil or probe_entry[key_products] != nil or probe_entry[key_products_legacy] != nil or probe_entry[key_collection] != nil
        assign fields_matched = true
      endif
    endfor
  -%}
  {%- if containing_product != blank and emitted_entries == 0 and lookbook_entries != blank -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No look features this product yet.</strong>
      This section shows the looks that already include the product being
      viewed, so nothing is shown until <em>{{ containing_product.title }}</em>
      is added to a lookbook entry — either to its picked products, or to the
      collection an entry points at. It stays hidden on the live storefront
      rather than rendering an empty heading.
    </div>
  {%- elsif lookbook_entries == blank and entry_source == 'selected' -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No entries picked yet.</strong>
      Use the Entries picker in this section to choose which looks to show
      and what order they appear in, or set Entries to show back to
      All entries.
    </div>
  {%- elsif lookbook_entries == blank -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No lookbook entries found.</strong>
      Check that the metaobject type handle is <code>lookbook</code>, that
      Storefronts API access is enabled on the definition, and that at least
      one entry has status Active rather than Draft.
    </div>
  {%- elsif fields_matched == false -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>Found {{ lookbook_entries.size }} entries, but no fields matched.</strong>
      This snippet is looking for the field keys
      <code>{{ key_title }}</code>, <code>{{ key_subheading }}</code>,
      <code>{{ key_description }}</code>, <code>{{ key_template }}</code>,
      <code>{{ key_products }}</code> (or <code>{{ key_products_legacy }}</code>),
      <code>{{ key_collection }}</code> and <code>{{ key_products_source }}</code>.
      Open the metaobject definition, expand each field to see its real key,
      then update the keys at the top of <code>snippets/lookbook.liquid</code>.
    </div>
  {%- elsif has_missing_collection -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>An entry is set to Collection but has no collection picked.</strong>
      Open that entry in Settings &gt; Custom data and either choose a
      collection, or set its Products Source back to the picked-products
      option.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
